<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  palai-agentd — the root daemon that owns session accounts on a Mac.

  INSTALL PATH: /Library/LaunchDaemons/net.pallasite.palai-agentd.plist, root:wheel, mode 0644.
  Task 2 of this phase installs it; this task does not put anything on a machine.

  WHY A DAEMON AND NOT sudo. There are a hundred of these machines and nobody types a password on
  them. A sudoers line needs one, silently rewrites the environment (secure_path once made the same
  command run BSD `find` under sudo and `bfs` in a shell in this repository), and grants everything the
  named binary can be argued into doing. This grants one socket and a three-word verb set.

  ‼️ THIS IS RunAtLoad + KeepAlive, NOT SOCKET ACTIVATION, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS DELIBERATE.
  Being handed a listener on demand needs `launch_activate_socket`, a C function in libSystem with no
  wrapper in the standard library or in golang.org/x/sys. Calling it needs cgo, and this tree has zero
  cgo: every release build is CGO_ENABLED=0 and CI is ubuntu-24.04, so a cgo daemon is a daemon CI
  never compiles. What is kept: launchd starts this at boot, restarts it when it dies, and "is it up?"
  stops being a question. What is lost: the socket does not exist until the daemon has bound it, so a
  caller racing a cold boot gets ECONNREFUSED rather than a queued connection.

  ‼️ AND THAT WINDOW IS WHY TASK 2's PROBE RETRIES RATHER THAN ASKING ONCE. The window opens exactly on
  a freshly booted cloud Mac — which is the zero-touch path itself, the one nobody is watching. A
  single-shot probe there reads "no daemon" and keeps the machine out of the pool: fail-closed is the
  right direction, but triggered by the wrong cause, and a machine that is fine would be refused for
  being early. So the probe backs off a bounded number of times and refuses where the retries run out.
  The window is a ceiling; it is not an UNCLOSED one, and the thing that closes it is Task 2.

  THE SOCKET IS THE CREDENTIAL. There is no password, no token and no TTY between a caller and root
  here: membership of group `palai` is the entire authorisation. The daemon therefore refuses to serve
  any socket that is not exactly mode 0660 and group `palai` — a permission that protects root is
  checked, not assumed. Create the group with no members and add exactly the account the control plane
  runs as:

      sudo dseditgroup -o create -r "Palai agent clients" palai
      sudo dseditgroup -o edit -a <control-plane-user> -t user palai

  Note for whoever writes the socket-activation version later: launchd's SockPathGroup and
  SockPathOwner take INTEGERS (a gid and a uid, not names) and SockPathMode is a DECIMAL integer,
  because property lists have no octal — 0660 is 432. Its own man page calls that a known bug.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>net.pallasite.palai-agentd</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/usr/local/libexec/palai-agentd</string>
		<string>-socket</string>
		<string>/var/run/palai-agentd.sock</string>
		<string>-group</string>
		<string>palai</string>
	</array>

	<!-- Root, because creating and deleting accounts is what this is for. It drops nothing and holds
	     nothing else: the verb set is the privilege. -->
	<key>UserName</key>
	<string>root</string>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Unconditional restart. A daemon that died leaves a machine that cannot isolate a session, and
	     an unisolated session is the failure this whole phase exists to prevent — so the answer to a
	     crash is to come back, not to stay down. -->
	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- 10s between respawns. Without it a daemon that refuses its socket posture on every start (see
	     the mode check) would spin, and the log line naming what is wrong would scroll past. -->
	<key>ThrottleInterval</key>
	<integer>10</integer>

	<!-- The environment is NOT inherited from whoever installed this, which is half the reason this
	     replaces sudo. It is stated here so that what the daemon runs with is a thing somebody read. -->
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
	</dict>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/palai-agentd.log</string>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/palai-agentd.log</string>

	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Adaptive</string>
</dict>
</plist>
